// A `@!prelude` outside the package that provides the prelude is inert.
//
// Unlike `@lang`, which is an error to claim (there is one answer to "what does
// owning a resource mean" and a package must not take it over), providing a
// prelude is a perfectly ordinary thing for a package to do - and that same
// package is just a library to any program that depends on it. Its mark must
// not follow it in and displace the prelude of the program consuming it. So a
// mark the compiler did not ask for is ignored rather than rejected.
//
// If it were honoured here, this module would be its own prelude and `println`
// would be an unknown function.

@!prelude

@export
proc main() i32 {
    println("inert");
    return 0;
}
